'''Auckland Central''' is a New Zealand [[New Zealand electorates|electoral division]] returning one member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The electorate is currently represented by [[Chlöe Swarbrick]], a member of the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]]; she has represented the seat since [[Auckland Central in the 2020 New Zealand general election|2020]].

==Population centres==
In the 1887 electoral redistribution, although the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Representation Commission]] was required through the Representation Act 1887 to maintain existing electorates "as far as possible", rapid population growth in the [[North Island]] required the transfer of three seats from the [[South Island]] to the north. Ten new electorates were created, including Auckland Central, and one former electorate was recreated.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=50–53}}

Auckland Central contains the [[Auckland city centre]], the suburbs of [[Ponsonby, New Zealand|Ponsonby]], [[Westmere, New Zealand|Westmere]], [[Arch Hill, New Zealand|Arch Hill]], [[Herne Bay, New Zealand|Herne Bay]], [[Freemans Bay]], [[Saint Marys Bay, New Zealand|St Mary's Bay]], [[Mechanics Bay]], [[Newton, New Zealand|Newton]] and [[Eden Terrace]] at the west side of the city. Because of the location of the main Auckland [[ferry]] terminal, Auckland Central also contains the islands of the [[Hauraki Gulf]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/auckland-central-electorate-profile/ |title=Auckland Central - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 21 September 2025}}</ref>

==History==
Auckland Central was created ahead of the [[1887 New Zealand general election|1887 election]]; it was carved from parts of the electorates of [[Auckland North (New Zealand electorate)|Auckland North]] and the [[Auckland West]] and focused around upper [[Queen Street, Auckland|Queen Street]], [[Grafton, New Zealand|Grafton]], and [[Newton, New Zealand|Newton]]. It lasted only until the [[1890 New Zealand general election|1890 elections]], when a reduction in the number of electorates meant Auckland Central was re-incorporated into a larger [[City of Auckland (New Zealand electorate)|City of Auckland electorate]]. At the [[1905 New Zealand general election|1905 elections]], the Auckland seat was split into three seats, including a recreated Auckland Central.

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Auckland Central.map|frame-longitude=174.75|frame-latitude=-36.85|zoom=12|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections (including islands on the [[Hauraki Gulf]])}}
Following the 2007 boundary review, Grafton became part of {{NZ electorate link|Epsom}} and [[Point Chevalier]] moved into {{NZ electorate link|Mount Albert}}, while in order to offset these changes the suburb of Newton was drafted in from Mount Albert. Further population growth ahead of the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 election]] resulted in [[Westmere, New Zealand|Westmere]] and [[Grey Lynn]] being transferred to Mount Albert in the 2014 boundary review.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Representation Commission|date=2014|title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014|url=https://elections.nz/assets/2014-boundary-review/Representation-Commissions-report-2014.pdf|access-date=18 October 2020}}</ref> At the 2025 boundary review, the electorate gained Westmere, [[Western Springs (Auckland suburb)|Western Springs]] and Grey Lynn from Mount Albert, and lost part of [[Arch Hill, New Zealand|Arch Hill]] to Mount Albert and Grafton and Newton to Epsom.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=17 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

The seat has been held by the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] for most of its existence: between 1919 and 2008, the seat had spent only three years in the hands of another party (the left-wing [[Alliance (New Zealand political party)|Alliance]], from 1993 to 1996). However, the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]] saw [[Nikki Kaye]] win the seat for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] for the first time. Kaye retained the seat in 2011, 2014 and 2017, although with a reduced majority making Auckland Central one of the most marginal electorates in the country. She retired at the 2020 election, which saw [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] candidate [[Chlöe Swarbrick]] win the seat in a tight three-way contest and become only the second Green electorate MP.

